# Source: Slides "ICT3112 - 7 - Recepy for Loop Invariants - Example 2" (powers of two).
id: powers-of-two
title: Powers of two
topic: Loop invariants
level: 2
source: Slides 7, Recipe for Loop Invariants, Example 2
tags: recipe, invariant, trace, variant

task:
  The loop computes $2^n$ by doubling $r$, $n$ times. Find a loop
  invariant $I$ by tracing the values of $i$ and $r$ at the loop head.
  Then prove initialisation, preservation and conclusion. Finally, give a
  variant and show that the loop terminates.

given:
  Loop condition B: $i < n$

pre: n >= 0
post: r = 2^n

program:
  {P}
  {R2}
  r := 1;            // i1
  {R1}
  i := 0;            // i2
  {I}
  while (i < n) do {
    {I && i < n}
    {R4}
    r := 2 * r;      // b1
    {R3}
    i := i + 1       // b2
    {I}
  }
  {I && !(i < n)}
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: The invariant I
rule: WHILE-CONSEQUENCE
focus: while
text:
  | At the loop head | $i$ | $r$ |
  |---|---|---|
  | before iteration 1 | $0$ | $1$ |
  | before iteration 2 | $1$ | $2$ |
  | before iteration 3 | $2$ | $4$ |
  | before iteration 4 | $3$ | $8$ |

  The trace gives $r = 2^i$. It is the postcondition $Q$ with $n$
  replaced by the counter $i$. We add the bounds $0 <= i <= n$ of the
  counter: at the exit they turn $!(i < n)$ into $i = n$.
set: I = r = 2^i && 0 <= i <= n
text:
  The loop rule with consequence has three premises, with the loop
  condition $B$ being $i < n$: initialisation, preservation and
  conclusion.

step: R1, the weakest precondition of i := 0 for I
part: Initialisation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i2
text:
  The invariant must hold when the loop is reached. The loop is preceded
  by `r := 1; i := 0`, so initialisation is the triple
  >>> {«P»} r := 1; i := 0 {«I»}
  We compute its weakest precondition from the last statement. We
  substitute $i$ with $0$ in the postcondition of `i := 0`, the invariant
  $I$.
calc:
  wp(i := 0, I)
  = (r = 2^i && 0 <= i <= n)[i <- 0]
  = r = 2^0 && 0 <= 0 <= n       // every i is replaced by 0
  = r = 1 && 0 <= 0 <= n         // 2^0 = 1
  = r = 1 && 0 <= n              // 0 <= 0 is true, so only 0 <= n is left
set: R1 = r = 1 && 0 <= n

step: R2, the weakest precondition of r := 1 for R1
part: Initialisation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i1
text:
  We substitute $r$ with $1$ in the postcondition of this statement, which
  is $R1$.
calc:
  wp(r := 1, R1)
  = (r = 1 && 0 <= n)[r <- 1]
  = 1 = 1 && 0 <= n              // every r is replaced by 1
  = 0 <= n                       // 1 = 1 is true, and true && A is A
set: R2 = 0 <= n
note:
  The initial value of $r$ matters. With `r := 0`, $R2$ would be
  $0 = 1 && 0 <= n$, which is false.

step: P implies R2
part: Initialisation
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, R2
text:
  $R2$ is the weakest precondition of `r := 1; i := 0` for $I$. By the
  consequence rule, initialisation holds when the precondition $P$ implies
  $R2$.
  >>> «P» ==> «R2»
vc: n >= 0 ==> 0 <= n
why:
  Both sides say the same thing.

step: R3, the weakest precondition of i := i + 1 for I
part: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: b2
text:
  One iteration must keep the invariant:
  >>> {«I» && «i < n : B»} r := 2 * r; i := i + 1 {«I»}
  We compute the weakest precondition of the body for $I$, from the last
  statement. We substitute $i$ with $i + 1$ in $I$.
calc:
  wp(i := i + 1, I)
  = (r = 2^i && 0 <= i <= n)[i <- i + 1]
  = r = 2^(i + 1) && 0 <= i + 1 <= n     // every i is replaced by i + 1
set: R3 = r = 2^(i + 1) && 0 <= i + 1 <= n

step: R4, the weakest precondition of r := 2 * r for R3
part: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: b1
text:
  We substitute $r$ with $2 * r$ in $R3$.
calc:
  wp(r := 2 * r, R3)
  = (r = 2^(i + 1) && 0 <= i + 1 <= n)[r <- 2 * r]
  = 2 * r = 2^(i + 1) && 0 <= i + 1 <= n     // every r is replaced by 2 * r
set: R4 = 2 * r = 2^(i + 1) && 0 <= i + 1 <= n

step: I and B imply R4
part: Preservation
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: I && i < n, R4
text:
  $R4$ is the weakest precondition of the body for $I$. By the consequence
  rule, preservation holds when $I$ and $B$ together imply $R4$.
  >>> «I» && «i < n : B» ==> «R4»
vc: r = 2^i && 0 <= i <= n && i < n ==> 2 * r = 2^(i + 1) && 0 <= i + 1 <= n
why:
  First conjunct: $r = 2^i$ by $I$, so $2 * r = 2 * 2^i = 2^(i + 1)$.

  Second conjunct: $0 <= i$ gives $0 <= i + 1$, and $i < n$ gives
  $i + 1 <= n$.

step: I and not B imply Q
part: Conclusion
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
focus: I && !(i < n), Q
text:
  When the loop stops, $I$ holds and the loop condition $B$ is false. This
  must imply the postcondition $Q$.
  >>> «I» && «!(i < n) : ¬B» ==> «Q»
vc: r = 2^i && 0 <= i <= n && !(i < n) ==> r = 2^n
why:
  $!(i < n)$ is $i >= n$. With $i <= n$ from $I$ it gives $i = n$.
  Replacing $i$ by $n$ in $r = 2^i$ gives $r = 2^n$.

step: The variant is not negative
part: Termination
rule: WHILE-TOTAL
focus: I && i < n
text:
  The counter $i$ goes up to $n$, so $n - i$ is the number of iterations
  left. We take the variant $E = n - i$. The rule WHILE-TOTAL has two
  premises. The first one: $E$ is not negative when an iteration starts.
  >>> «I» && «i < n : B» ==> «n - i >= 0 : E >= 0»
vc: r = 2^i && 0 <= i <= n && i < n ==> n - i >= 0
why:
  $i < n$ from $B$ gives $n - i > 0$.

step: The variant after i := i + 1
part: Termination
rule: WHILE-TOTAL, ASSIGN, WP
focus: b2
text:
  The second premise: the body makes $E$ smaller. $E0$ is a logical
  variable for the value of $E$ when the iteration starts.
  >>> {«I» && «i < n : B» && «n - i = E0 : E = E0»} r := 2 * r; i := i + 1 {«n - i < E0 : E < E0»}
  We compute the weakest precondition of the body for $n - i < E0$, from
  the last statement. We substitute $i$ with $i + 1$.
calc:
  wp(i := i + 1, n - i < E0)
  = (n - i < E0)[i <- i + 1]
  = n - (i + 1) < E0          // i is replaced by i + 1

step: The variant after r := 2 * r
part: Termination
rule: WHILE-TOTAL, ASSIGN, WP
focus: b1
text:
  We substitute $r$ with $2 * r$ in the result of the previous step.
calc:
  wp(r := 2 * r, n - (i + 1) < E0)
  = (n - (i + 1) < E0)[r <- 2 * r]
  = n - (i + 1) < E0          // r does not occur, so nothing changes

step: The body decreases the variant
part: Termination
rule: WHILE-TOTAL, CONSEQUENCE
focus: I && i < n
text:
  By the consequence rule, the second premise holds when $I$, $B$ and
  $E = E0$ imply the weakest precondition just computed.
  >>> «I» && «i < n : B» && «n - i = E0 : E = E0» ==> «n - (i + 1) < E0 : wp»
vc: r = 2^i && 0 <= i <= n && i < n && n - i = E0 ==> n - (i + 1) < E0
why:
  $n - (i + 1) = (n - i) - 1 = E0 - 1$, which is smaller than $E0$.
result: The triple is valid, and the loop terminates.
